WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Process Redesign and the Five Sigma Wall

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.533
Welcome back to this session on performance benchmarks.

3
00:00:08.533 --> 00:00:12.733
What we have here is a set of benchmarking data.

4
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:19.066
What I’d like to emphasize here, is this is the Z value's based on raw data,

5
00:00:19.066 --> 00:00:24.599
Z.st or short-term Sigma capability.

6
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:30.233
Now, we see that in graphical form, but I want to focus on the histogram.

7
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:30.266
So, that we can see this chart in a little more pure form.

8
00:00:30.266 --> 00:00:36.066
Now this is very interesting. Now, what we have is on the horizontal scale is Sigma capability

9
00:00:36.066 --> 00:00:39.066
and the vertical scale is just frequency count.

10
00:00:39.066 --> 00:00:42.799
So we're looking at a histogram of the Sigma’s, if you will,

11
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:46.233
across that wave of master black belts.

12
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:50.566
We, see this same graph over and over. What I want to focus on, is look at this.

13
00:00:50.566 --> 00:00:54.566
The modal condition is 4, the average is about 3-1/2.

14
00:00:54.566 --> 00:00:59.766
Very typical of what we see. But look out here in this tail.

15
00:00:59.766 --> 00:01:06.766
Wow, it suddenly builds up in Sigma and then quickly falls off, starts to shoot up again.

16
00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:12.199
This is what I call the 5 Sigma wall.

17
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.533
You can see it's almost as if there is an invisible wall about five Sigma.

18
00:01:17.533 --> 00:01:23.666
That is suddenly truncating, what would otherwise be a normal distribution.

19
00:01:23.666 --> 00:01:27.666
Well here is why that exists.

20
00:01:27.666 --> 00:01:33.499
We can improve up to the limit of our ability to measure.

21
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.433
And we tend to measure, about one digit further

22
00:01:38.433 --> 00:01:41.399
than what we control.

23
00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:46.266
So, once we have improved, we've now lost

24
00:01:46.266 --> 00:01:49.899
our granularity to improve further.

25
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:55.966
In other words, we can no longer detect more beneficial change.

26
00:01:55.966 --> 00:01:59.399
And that's the five Sigma wall.

27
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.400
It also tends to be related to the limit of the design.

28
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.833
The design capability is about five.

29
00:02:07.833 --> 00:02:12.366
The production capability is about 3-1/2, on average.

30
00:02:12.366 --> 00:02:17.732
So we’ve got about a 1 1/2 Sigma differential.

31
00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:22.433
Then we have a few out here around six.

32
00:02:22.433 --> 00:02:27.433
Which says this, sometimes we achieve Six Sigma even by mistake.

33
00:02:27.433 --> 00:02:31.433
We get it, if there is enough activity going on.

34
00:02:31.433 --> 00:02:35.833
But nonetheless, take a look at that histogram. We tend to see that over and over.

35
00:02:35.833 --> 00:02:41.533
This basically says that in order to go past

36
00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:47.166
Five Sigma, we’ve got to redesign the product or service.

37
00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:52.166
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
